//! Headless-compositor end-to-end smoke tests for the real `wolf-gnome` window.
//!
//! ## What it proves
//!
//! That the built `wolf-gnome` binary launches a real GTK/Adwaita window under a
//! headless Wayland compositor, attaches Neovim, accepts keyboard input, and
//! *actually renders glyphs* — verified by screenshotting the live window and
//! OCR-ing it. Unlike the Tier-1 seam tests (which exercise `gdk_key`/`paint`
//! offscreen), the cage smokes here are the only tests that confirm the whole stack
//! draws a window a human could see.
//!
//! ## What this file contains
//!
//! **Fifty-three cage smokes** — one window-render smoke, two file-picker smokes,
//! one file-tree-sidebar smoke, five split-pane smokes, one empty-state smoke, one
//! new-untitled-buffer smoke, one command-palette smoke, one OPEN RECENT smoke
//! (Ctrl+Shift+P, a typed folder query, then Enter — the activated row re-roots the
//! window through the same `shell::chdir_to` the native Open Folder dialog takes,
//! which the captive Neovim's own `getcwd()` readback and the re-rooted sidebar are
//! what prove), two BUFFER-PICKER smokes
//! (Ctrl+Shift+E then a typed query, switching to the queried buffer; and
//! Ctrl+Shift+E then a BARE Enter, switching to the most-recently-used OTHER buffer
//! through the row-1 preselection), one PROJECT-SEARCH smoke (Ctrl+Shift+F, a typed
//! query, then TWO Enters — the first runs ripgrep, the second opens the matched
//! LINE, which the captive Neovim's own `expand('%:t')` / `line('.')` / `col('.')`
//! readback is what proves), one
//! Ctrl+Shift+W
//! close-pane smoke (the accelerator path, as opposed to the split-pane suite's
//! `<C-w>c`), two quit smokes (`:q`
//! closes the focused pane, `:qa` closes the window), one keyboard-only CRASH /
//! auto-restart smoke, one keyboard-only STALL smoke (`SIGSTOP` the captive Neovim,
//! then make the buffer picker's blocking RPC elapse its budget so the
//! "stopped responding" overlay is raised), seven POINTER smokes
//! (click-to-position, drag-to-select,
//! wheel scroll, the click-then-close focus-wedge regression, the crash
//! overlay + "Restart Neovim" button, a divider drag surviving a pane
//! re-layout, and a click on a LINK inside a rendered preview — the only injected
//! point in this file aimed at something WebKit drew, whose click constant is
//! re-derived from its own capture before it is used and whose evidence is a
//! `tabpagebuflist()` readback naming the file the pane was repointed at), one
//! keyboard-only NAVIGATION-HISTORY smoke (Alt+Left returning the
//! pane to the previously-open file, which the captive Neovim's own `bufname('%')`
//! readbacks either side of the chord are what prove), one keyboard-only
//! SIDEBAR-TOGGLE smoke (Ctrl+Shift+B hiding the file tree, read off TWO captures
//! so that neither a tree that never populated nor a window that stopped drawing
//! can satisfy the must-not-contain half), one keyboard-only TYPED-`:cd` smoke (a
//! relative `:cd ..` re-rooting the whole window on Neovim's OWN working directory,
//! which the captive Neovim's `getcwd()` readback plus two ordered OCR
//! discriminators — a file that exists only in the new root, and the synthetic root
//! row bearing the new cwd's basename — are what prove), one keyboard-only PER-PANE
//! STATUSBAR smoke (a vertical split with a different file open in each pane, whose
//! two strips must draw two DIFFERENT filenames — the crossed pair is what makes that
//! more than "a filename is on screen" — plus the captive Neovim's own
//! `winheight(0)` readback, which is the only end-to-end number the editor inset and
//! the strip's height reach), five
//! STATE-PERSISTENCE smokes (a
//! three-pane layout, a lone open file, and a dragged divider's WEIGHTS each
//! surviving a quit-and-relaunch, plus the window title rendering the working
//! directory, plus one TWO-WINDOW smoke — a second window opened from the palette
//! inside ONE process, quit with Ctrl+Shift+Q, and both sessions restored at
//! relaunch, whose evidence is the count of live `nvim` children of the app pid and
//! a frozen two-window `state.json` rather than any pixel, because `cage` shows one
//! toplevel at a time and declines a `present()` on the other), one
//! CONFIGURED-FONT-SIZE smoke (two launches at two
//! `wolf-config.yaml` font sizes, each writing `winwidth(0)` into its own file, so
//! the configured size reaching the cell metrics is an integer strict inequality
//! rather than an OCR judgement), one COLUMN-CAP smoke (one launch at a
//! deliberately SMALL font size, the only geometry at which the 90-column content
//! cap binds at all under `cage`, whose two `winwidth(0)` readbacks either side of
//! `:WolfFullWidth` are what prove the cap and the per-buffer override that lifts
//! it), one CONFIGURED-APPEARANCE smoke (two launches at two `wolf-config.yaml`
//! `appearance` values, each writing `&background` into its own file, so the
//! configured appearance reaching the captive Neovim is a pair of exact string
//! equalities — and the PAIR is what discriminates, since `dark` alone is satisfied
//! by a build that pushes nothing and `light` alone by one that always pushes
//! light), one CLIPBOARD smoke (a linewise yank over Ctrl+Shift+C, a put over
//! Ctrl+Shift+V, and one buffer readback that must carry the marker exactly TWICE),
//! and one MARKDOWN-PREVIEW smoke (Preview and then Edit Source driven through the
//! command palette, which is one of their only two routes: TWO captures either side
//! of a LIVE EDIT typed into the source, so the second reads text that reached the
//! DOM through the JSON body swap and no other way, plus a preview-only statusbar, a
//! frozen `state.json` whose preview leaf carries the SOURCE path, and two
//! `tabpagebuflist()` readbacks that name the preview scratch buffer), one
//! PREVIEW-APPEARANCE smoke (two launches at the two `wolf-config.yaml`
//! `appearance` values, each opening the same document into a preview pane, whose
//! evidence is RAW PIXELS decoded out of its own captures), one
//! UNSAVED-BUFFER PREVIEW smoke (Ctrl+Shift+N, a marker typed into a buffer that is
//! never written to disk, Preview through the palette, and then the source pane
//! covered by another file — so the readback naming `Preview: Untitled 2.md` and the
//! marker on screen together say the anonymous-buffer naming and the preview path
//! compose), one CODE-BLOCK-HIGHLIGHT smoke (one `go` fence and one
//! unknown-language fence carrying BYTE-IDENTICAL text, whose evidence is a
//! chromatic-pixel count in the first against an in-capture control count in the
//! second — an unhighlighted block is neutral on neutral — plus a Neovim readback
//! naming the INJECTED `go` langtree, which is the editor-side half and a different
//! feat-list row), one INLINE-CODE GRID smoke (three paragraphs each carrying an
//! inline code element above a full-width rule, whose evidence is the pixel distance
//! from the document's own top edge down to that rule, and the count of exact chip
//! pixels inside the first paragraph — two assertions with two DIFFERENT
//! falsifiers), one PICKER-OVER-PREVIEW smoke (the file picker's floating box opened
//! over a LIVE Markdown preview and photographed while it is still up — the only
//! capture in this file taken with a transient overlay open on purpose — whose
//! evidence is the same self-located overlap rectangle read on TWO frames of ONE
//! launch: the preview's background and its heading's ink in the control, and zero of
//! either plus the box's own fill in the sample), one COMPLETION-POPUP smoke, one
//! MULTI-LINE MESSAGE smoke (a two-line
//! `:echo`, whose LAST line is the control and whose FIRST line is the assertion),
//! one CONFIRM-PROMPT smoke (a `:confirm` question drawn and then cleared once
//! answered) and three TERMINAL-DRAWER smokes (Ctrl+` opening a full-width shell
//! strip below the editor: one reading a MARKER its own bash printed, out of a
//! narrow band cropped from the drawer strip — the statusbar above it draws a
//! per-run pid — beside a frozen `state.json` that must hold exactly one leaf and no
//! `term://`, and four `&buftype` readbacks covering both halves of the focus bridge,
//! a pane-area rebuild and the split guard; one reading four geometry integers at its
//! OWN small font size, because the 90-column cap the drawer is exempt from cannot
//! bind at the shared one; and one killing the captive Neovim with the drawer open,
//! whose discriminator is that `<C-w>k` can leave the RE-OPENED drawer at all)
//! — each of which launches the real binary under `cage`.
//!
//! Seven smokes run **two app launches inside ONE `cage` instance** (one scenario
//! string, two sequential `"$WOLF_BIN" &` blocks) — the four relaunch ones, the
//! configured-font-size one, the configured-appearance one and the
//! preview-appearance one — so `CAGE_LOCK`
//! semantics are unchanged and the count of compositors started is still one per
//! smoke. That shape is not merely economical for the two-window smoke, it is
//! MANDATORY there: [`cage_command`] removes `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`, so a
//! second `wolf-gnome` PROCESS becomes its own primary instance with its own
//! `StateStore` over the same directory rather than a second window of the first.
//!
//! Eight of them additionally run **`wolf-pointer-inject`** inside cage —
//! this repo's own long-lived `zwlr_virtual_pointer_v1` client, see
//! `tools/wolf-pointer-inject/README.md` — started BEFORE the app so the seat
//! already carries `WL_SEAT_CAPABILITY_POINTER` when GTK does its first `wl_seat`
//! roundtrip, and driven over a FIFO with a poll-for-ack protocol. Five of them
//! assert on FILES THE CAPTIVE NEOVIM WRITES ABOUT ITS OWN STATE — `line('.')` /
//! `col('.')`, `visualmode()` plus `'<` / `'>`, `line('w0')`, the per-window
//! `winwidth()`, and the `tabpagebuflist()` names either side of a preview-link
//! click — rather than on OCR. Those are exact values that no sidebar row
//! and no `:e` toast can satisfy, and OCR is structurally incapable of answering
//! "did the cursor land at exactly row 12, column 25".
//!
//! Thirty-six of the fifty-three OCR a screenshot of the live window, and exactly one
//! of those OCRs a CROP of it rather than the whole file: the terminal-drawer smoke
//! samples a band inside the drawer strip, because the statusbar directly above it
//! draws a `term://…//<pid>:…` string that differs on every run. Eight
//! deliberately capture no image at all: the `:qa` smoke asserts on the cage exit
//! status alone, while the configured-font-size and column-cap smokes add two
//! integers each, the configured-appearance smoke two exact strings, the
//! clipboard smoke one buffer readback (the
//! app must be GONE, and gone with a ZERO exit status — a panic in the GTK main
//! loop makes a process vanish just as a clean shutdown does), the drawer
//! full-width and drawer restart smokes read `&buftype` and four geometry integers,
//! and the two-window smoke counts live `nvim` children and reads a frozen
//! `state.json` — the only one of the eight for which a capture is not merely
//! unnecessary but IMPOSSIBLE, since `cage` shows a single toplevel and declines a
//! `present()` on any other, so no image could show two windows and none could
//! bring the first one back;
//! the click, drag,
//! scroll and divider-relayout smokes still capture and still RETAIN a screenshot
//! for post-mortem, but assert on their readback files rather than on pixels. The
//! three OCR-ing relaunch smokes OCR **and** assert on a Neovim readback, and two of
//! them additionally assert on the `state.json` the first launch wrote. The
//! project-search smoke does both as well, and its readback carries the two values
//! OCR structurally cannot: the LINE and the COLUMN the cursor landed on. The
//! markdown-preview and terminal-drawer smokes do all three at once — a capture, a
//! readback and a `state.json` frozen mid-run — because in both cases the surface
//! under test cannot be read any other way.
//!
//! Five of the fifty-three read RAW PIXELS out of their own captures, which is the
//! only route this harness has to a claim OCR cannot make — it reads no colour, and
//! it cannot answer "where on the page is this".
//! [`preview_document_follows_the_configured_appearance_under_cage`] captures two
//! images, runs `tesseract` over neither, and samples exact colours at a derived
//! rectangle, which is how it tells light-on-dark from dark-on-light.
//! [`preview_highlights_a_fenced_code_block_under_cage`] locates two rendered code
//! blocks by their own background and counts CHROMATIC pixels in each, which is how
//! it tells a highlighted block from a plain one without counting distinct colours.
//! [`preview_inline_code_keeps_the_baseline_grid_under_cage`] measures a DISTANCE in
//! pixel rows between two self-located landmarks, which is the only laid-out-box
//! reading either suite takes, and counts exact chip pixels for the paint claim.
//! [`preview_link_click_repoints_the_pane_under_cage`] decodes ONE of its two captures
//! and locates the rendered link's ink in it, purely to re-assert that its measured
//! click constant still names the link before it uses it; its own evidence is a Neovim
//! readback.
//! [`file_picker_covers_the_live_preview_under_cage`] decodes BOTH of its captures and
//! reads one self-located rectangle in each: it is the only smoke here whose two
//! frames differ solely by an overlay, and the only one that reads a widget's fill
//! colour out of the capture rather than naming it — which is what lets it locate a
//! private box's geometry without pinning it.
//!
//! **Seven pure guards**, which mechanically enforce invariants the cage smokes
//! depend on but cannot check themselves:
//!
//! * [`sidebar_filler_names_cannot_satisfy_any_smoke_assertion`] — no
//!   [`SIDEBAR_FILLER_FILES`] name may contain a term any smoke asserts on.
//! * [`temp_cwd_name_cannot_satisfy_any_smoke_assertion`] — the same rule aimed at a
//!   second fixture, the fixed components of the temp-cwd path
//!   ([`TEMP_CWD_BASENAME`] and [`TEMP_CWD_PREFIX`]), because the window title
//!   renders the app's working directory and the temp-dir NAME is therefore page
//!   text too. The path is deliberately shaped so the title is the CONSTANT
//!   `~/project` on every run — see [`make_temp_cwd`] and [`cage_command`] for the
//!   measured reason.
//! * [`restore_fixture_names_cannot_satisfy_their_own_assertions`] — the same rule
//!   aimed at a THIRD fixture, [`RELAUNCH_FIXTURE_NAMES`]: the SMOKE-OWNED files a
//!   smoke writes into its own working directory (the state-persistence ones were
//!   the first), which the tree sidebar renders by name. It pins more than names,
//!   because for the four fixtures whose asserted text is a BODY rather than a
//!   filename a name rule cannot reach the hazard at all: it checks the content of
//!   [`divider_right_fixture_body`] and [`search_target_fixture_body`], the second
//!   down to the match's line index and its byte offset, which the project-search
//!   smoke's line and column assertions are derived from, the markdown-preview
//!   source, whose rendered text is the one body that reaches a pixel through WebKit
//!   and the only one checked against an ordinary English word
//!   ([`PREVIEW_OCR_STATUS`]), and the unsaved-buffer preview smoke's marker and cover
//!   heading, whose asserted text lives in NO FILE AT ALL — it is typed into a buffer
//!   that is never written, so the name rule cannot reach it even in principle. It
//!   also pins SEVEN crossed
//!   pairs of OCR preconditions — the window-title smoke's deliberate exemption, the
//!   project-search smoke's target-versus-starting headings, the Open Recent
//!   smoke's target FILE versus target DIRECTORY, where the header bar draws the
//!   directory on every capture, the sidebar-toggle smoke's tree-row subject
//!   versus its proof-of-life heading, which must come from opposite halves of the
//!   window or hiding one would remove the other, the typed-`:cd` smoke's three
//!   fixed DIRECTORY components, whose crossing is what stops a header-bar title that
//!   failed to abbreviate standing in for the synthetic root row, and the per-pane
//!   statusbar smoke's two FILENAMES, the only pair whose halves are both
//!   must-CONTAIN terms — uncrossed, one pane's strip would satisfy both assertions
//!   and "each pane draws its own file" would go unproven — and the unsaved-buffer
//!   preview smoke's marker versus its cover heading, drawn by two DIFFERENT
//!   renderers, where an uncrossed pair would let the editor grid satisfy a claim
//!   about what WebKit put on screen.
//! * [`cage_is_only_spawned_through_the_isolating_helper`] — `cage` is spawned in
//!   exactly one place, [`cage_command`], which is the only thing that sets
//!   `WOLF_STATE_DIR` and so the only thing keeping the smokes off the developer's
//!   real `~/.local/state/wolf/` — and, since the shader-cache fix, the only thing
//!   that sets `XDG_CACHE_HOME` too (see [`smoke_shader_cache_dir`]).
//! * [`the_column_cap_cannot_bind_at_the_ocr_smokes_geometry`] — the premise every
//!   OCR smoke's geometry rests on, given `editor_inset`'s content-width cap:
//!   at the pinned output size, the measured pane origin and
//!   `SMOKE_FONT_PT`, one pane is far too narrow for 90 columns, so the cap never
//!   binds, no grid is ever centred, and no pixel target moves. That premise is
//!   exactly the kind that stops being true when someone changes a font size, and
//!   nothing else would notice.
//! * [`the_shared_smoke_config_pins_the_appearance_every_ocr_smoke_assumes`] — the
//!   SECOND premise those thirty-six rest on: the seeded `wolf-config.yaml` pins
//!   `appearance: dark`, which is what keeps the grid light-on-dark for sparse-text
//!   segmentation. It asserts both halves — the pinned VALUE, re-typed locally so
//!   the check is not a comparison with itself, and the artifact
//!   [`write_smoke_config`] actually writes — because the pin and the assertions it
//!   can disarm sit thousands of lines apart, exactly the shape the three
//!   fixture-name guards above exist for.
//! * [`codehl_chroma_thresholds_are_separated`] — [`CODEHL_MIN_CHROMA_PX`] must stay
//!   at least five times [`CODEHL_MAX_CHROMA_PX`]. The code-block smoke's pair of
//!   assertions discriminates only while the floor sits well ABOVE the ceiling: set
//!   the floor at or below what the CONTROL block itself reads and a block that lost
//!   its highlighting entirely would still clear it, with nothing in the run naming
//!   the constant that disarmed it. It asserts on the SHORTFALL rather than on the
//!   comparison, because `clippy::assertions_on_constants` rejects an `assert!` whose
//!   whole condition is constant and an `#[allow]` would suppress that lint for
//!   anything added here later.
//!
//! All seven are compositor-free: none calls `require_smoke_env`, none takes
//! `CAGE_LOCK` and none spawns a subprocess, so they still guard on a machine where
//! the smoke toolchain is unavailable. They live here, next to the consts and
//! helpers they read, rather than in a file of their own.
//!
//! Six of the seven additionally need NO environment at all. The exception is
//! [`the_column_cap_cannot_bind_at_the_ocr_smokes_geometry`], and the claim is
//! stated precisely rather than loosely: it measures a
//! real cell through [`cell_metrics_for_smoke`], so it needs the devShell's pinned
//! `FONTCONFIG_FILE`. That is not part of the cage toolchain listed below — it is
//! the same font pinning `tests/paint_golden.rs` needs — but it is not nothing, and
//! a run outside the devShell fails it for that reason rather than for the reason
//! its message names.
//!
//! So this target's summary line reads **`60 passed`**, not `53`: fifty-three cage
//! smokes plus those seven guards.
//!
//! ## A vacuously-passing check recorded here: the `wtype ""` window-map poll
//!
//! Every scenario opens with a loop that retries a no-op `wtype ""` until one
//! succeeds, and its comment used to claim that success "only happens once cage has
//! a mapped surface to deliver input to". **That claim is measured FALSE.**
//! Instrumented, the loop breaks at **`tries = 0` on every single run**: `cage`
//! advertises `zwp_virtual_keyboard_manager_v1` as soon as the compositor itself is
//! up, BEFORE any client surface maps, so the very first `wtype ""` always succeeds
//! and the `WAIT_TRIES = 75` budget (15 s) is never spent.
//!
//! So the poll does **not** gate input readiness, and `WAIT_TRIES` / `WAIT_INTERVAL`
//! are not an input-readiness budget. The ONLY real budget between spawning the app
//! and typing the first key is the fixed `SETTLE` sleep that follows the loop —
//! which is why `SETTLE` carries the measured margin table in [`SCENARIO`] and why
//! it was raised from `0.5` to `1.0`.
//!
//! The poll is deliberately **kept**, not deleted: it still catches the one thing it
//! can catch — a `cage` that never came up at all — and its `kill -0` arm still
//! catches an app that died before the scenario ever typed. It simply no longer
//! claims something it cannot prove.
//!
//! This is the EIGHTH vacuously-passing check this effort has found. It is recorded
//! HERE, beside the scenarios, because that is where the next reader meets it — while
//! editing a scenario's poll knobs, not while reading Markdown;
//! `crates/wolf-gnome/tests/README.md` keeps the running prose record of the others,
//! under "A red-run demonstration is a deliverable, not a formality". It is the same
//! shape as the entries already there (the drag smoke's false premise, the untested
//! auto-restart cap, the centre-derived restart-button click): **a check aimed at the right
//! property, looking somewhere the defect could not appear.**
//!
//! Its concrete cost was DIAGNOSTIC. A residual ~0.8% failure of
//! [`window_renders_typed_text_under_cage`] (1 in 120) was chased as input routing
//! and then as the swap modal, because a poll that appeared to have already proved
//! the client could accept keys ruled out the true cause — too little margin before
//! the first keystroke — by fiat. The measured signature (two empty lines, cursor on
//! line 2) is now the FIRST thing that smoke's failure message tells the reader to
//! check.
//!
//! ## When it runs
//!
//! The fifty-three cage smokes run as part of the STANDARD suite — `cargo test -p
//! wolf-gnome` and `cargo test --workspace` — inside the flake devShell. They are
//! the Linux verification gate's only end-to-end proof that the GTK front-end draws
//! a window a human could see (see `build.md`, "The Linux verification gate").
//!
//! They are deliberately NOT `#[ignore]`d. "They are slow" is not a reason to skip
//! the only real end-to-end test the front-end has; per `CLAUDE.md` a test the
//! suite command does not run is a silently-skipped test.
//!
//! They are, however, the slowest part of the suite: each drives a real
//! compositor, and all fifty-three serialize behind `CAGE_LOCK` (see below).
//!
//! ```text
//! nix develop --command cargo test -p wolf-gnome --test smoke_headless
//! ```
//!
//! ## Requirements of the fifty-three cage smokes (from the devShell's smoke toolchain)
//!
//! - `cage` (headless wlroots compositor), `wtype`, `grim`, `tesseract` and
//!   `wolf-pointer-inject` on `PATH`. The last one is NOT an upstream package: it is
//!   built from this repo's own `tools/wolf-pointer-inject/` by `flake.nix`, so
//!   `command -v wolf-pointer-inject` succeeding can only mean the flake provided
//!   it. Only the eight pointer-driven smokes use it, but it is required
//!   unconditionally — see [`REQUIRED_TOOLS`].
//! - `WOLF_NVIM_BIN` pointing at the pinned Neovim (same contract as the other
//!   wolf-gnome tests; nvim is never taken from `PATH`).
//! - `WOLF_RG_BIN` pointing at the pinned ripgrep. `WolfCore::start_search` resolves
//!   it from that variable ALONE and never from `PATH`, so an unset one gives the
//!   project-search overlay an immediately-complete session with zero matches. See
//!   [`require_smoke_env`], which fails loud rather than letting that surface as an
//!   empty result list.
//! - `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` set and writable (the Wayland socket lives there).
//!
//! Per project policy every one of the fifty-three FAILS LOUD if any of the above is
//! missing rather than silently skipping, mirroring `common::require_nvim_bin` — a
//! missing precondition means the smoke toolchain was not provisioned, which is a
//! setup bug we want surfaced, not hidden. Now that these tests run by default, that
//! hard fail matters more, not less: a missing tool means the suite was invoked
//! outside the devShell, and softening `require_smoke_env` to a skip would let the
//! gate pass green on an unprovisioned machine while proving nothing. Do not.

use std::ffi::OsStr;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};
use std::process::{Command, ExitStatus};
use std::time::SystemTime;

use cairo::{Context, Format, ImageSurface};
use wolf_core::config::CONFIG_FILE_NAME;
use wolf_core::state::STATE_FILE_NAME;
use wolf_core::{
    load_state, preferences_dir, state_dir, CommandMru, PaneNode, PaneOrientation, RecentDirs,
};
use wolf_gnome::{CellMetrics, WINDOW_TITLE_FALLBACK};

/// The lowercase word we type in insert mode (in the window smoke scenario).
/// Kept short and all-lowercase so OCR noise is unlikely to corrupt every
/// character. After typing it we move the cursor OFF its row (see [`SCENARIO`])
/// so the full word — not just a prefix — can be asserted against OCR.
const OCR_WORD: &str = "hello";

/// Serializes the fifty-three cage smoke tests. Each launches its own
/// `cage` compositor, and two compositors started concurrently contend for the
/// same Wayland socket under `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` (lockfile error), failing both.
/// Under the default multi-threaded test harness these tests would otherwise run
/// in parallel, so each acquires this lock for its whole body to force serial
/// execution. Poisoning is recovered from (`into_inner`) so one panicking test
/// does not cascade into failing the others.
///
/// The eight pointer-driven smokes join this same lock rather than living in a file of
/// their own: each `tests/*.rs` is a separate crate AND a separate PROCESS, so an
/// in-process `static Mutex` cannot serialize across them at all, and
/// `tests/common/mod.rs` is compiled separately into every integration-test binary,
/// so a `static CAGE_LOCK` moved there would be N independent mutexes rather than
/// one shared one — it would look like a fix and serialize nothing.
static CAGE_LOCK: std::sync::Mutex<()> = std::sync::Mutex::new(());

/// Font size (points) handed to the app via `WOLF_FONT_PT`. Larger than the
/// interactive default so the rendered glyphs are big enough for `tesseract` to
/// recognize reliably in a screenshot.
const SMOKE_FONT_PT: &str = "28";

/// The `appearance` every smoke's seeded `wolf-config.yaml` carries — see
/// [`write_smoke_config`], whose doc comment holds the reason the key is PINNED at
/// all and the reason it is pinned to this value in particular.
///
/// Only the two-launch appearance smoke passes anything else, and it passes both
/// of the other values in one run.
const SMOKE_APPEARANCE: &str = "dark";

/// The `line-height` (percent of the font size) every smoke's seeded
/// `wolf-config.yaml` carries — see [`smoke_config_dir`], which writes it, and
/// [`smoke_line_height`], which parses it back for this file's own measurement.
///
/// ## Why this exact number
///
/// It is the value at which the configured cell height reproduces the
/// pre-config one EXACTLY, so no derived pixel target in this file moves.
/// Measured on this host with the pinned DejaVu Sans Mono at
/// [`SMOKE_FONT_PT`]: the Pango context over a 1x1 `ImageSurface` reports no
/// resolution, so `CellMetrics::for_font` takes its 96 dpi arm and
/// `size_px = 28 * 96 / 72 = 37.3333`; `ascent` is 35, `descent` is 9, and
/// `metrics.height()` is 44, so the clamped `leading` is exactly 0. The cell
/// height the app computes is therefore `round(37.3333 * 1.18) = round(44.05) =
/// 44`, which is `ascent + descent` — byte-identical to the height the app used
/// before it read a config at all.
///
/// Two consequences, both load-bearing. Every pointer target, the pane-origin
/// precondition and the scroll threshold are derived from that cell size, so all
/// of them are unmoved. And because `cell_h == ascent + descent`, the baseline
/// offset `render::draw_glyph` applies (`baseline - ascent`, which is
/// `(cell_h - ascent - descent) / 2`) is exactly `0.0`, so every glyph paints at
/// the same coordinates it did before and no OCR assertion's calibration changes.
///
/// `117` and `119` round to 44 as well; `118` is chosen because it is the middle
/// of that run (`37.3333 x 1.18 = 44.0533`, `0.447` clear of the 44.5 boundary and
/// `0.553` clear of 43.5), so it is the most robust of the three to a float
/// difference in a later Pango. `117` gives `43.68`, only `0.18` from a boundary —
/// do not use it.
///
/// A `0` here would also reproduce today's cell height at any size, since the
/// measured leading is zero and the non-positive branch is then
/// `ceil(ascent + descent)`. It is deliberately NOT used: a positive percentage
/// exercises the shipped code path these smokes exist to protect, and a `0` would
/// leave that path untouched by any smoke at all.
///
/// **Changing this moves every derived pixel target in this file.** Re-measure
/// before touching it; do not adjust it to make an assertion pass.
const SMOKE_LINE_HEIGHT: &str = "118";

/// How many tiny marker `.md` files [`window_renders_typed_text_under_cage`] drops
/// into its temp cwd before launching the app, purely so the tree sidebar has rows
/// of text to draw.
///
/// This is an OCR-robustness fixture, not a product requirement, and it weakens
/// nothing: the assertion, [`OCR_WORD`], [`SMOKE_FONT_PT`], the [`SCENARIO`] string
/// and its timing knobs are all untouched.
///
/// ## Why the sidebar has to carry text
///
/// With an EMPTY cwd the app renders correctly and the assertion still fails: the
/// capture shows row 0 reading `hello` with the block cursor exactly ONE row below
/// it, and an ASCII pixel dump of that region shows `h e l l o` with correct
/// ascenders and no clipping — yet `tesseract` reports `ﬁello`, misclassifying the
/// isolated leading `h` as the ligature U+FB01 (`ﬁ`) when the rest of the page is
/// nearly blank. The decisive control: cropping a PASSING capture (one taken with a
/// densely populated sidebar) down to just the editor region reproduces `ﬁello` from
/// the very same pixels. The `hello` glyphs are therefore byte-identical in the
/// passing and failing cases; the only variable is how much other text is on the page
/// for the classifier to calibrate against. `--psm 3`, `4`, `6`, `11` and `12` were
/// all tried and all fail on the sparse page, so this is not a page-segmentation-mode
/// question and must not be "fixed" by changing psm.
///
/// ## Why this number
///
/// Measured on this host: 5 files is the FLOOR at which OCR reads `hello` reliably
/// (1 file and 3 files both fail; 5 files passed three runs in a row, 12 files
/// passed). Nine sits comfortably above that floor without slowing the file-search
/// traversal. If this ever regresses, add files — do not touch the assertion.
///
/// ## The window title is page text too, and is deliberately held CONSTANT
///
/// Since the state-persistence unit the `adw::HeaderBar` renders the app's WORKING
/// DIRECTORY, so the title is part of the page `tesseract` calibrates against — a
/// third input to this fixture's arithmetic, alongside the filler rows and the
/// editor grid. It was measured to matter, and the measurement is recorded here
/// rather than left to be re-derived:
///
/// | arm | five consecutive capture md5s | size |
/// | --- | --- | --- |
/// | title rendering the run-varying temp path | all five DIFFERENT | 34.2–35.0 KB, varying |
/// | `set_title` suppressed (the base page) | `5ea4d070…` ×5, byte-identical | 26074 ×5 |
///
/// With a run-varying title — [`make_temp_cwd`] used to hand the app a directory
/// named from `std::env::temp_dir()` + the pid + a 19-digit nanosecond timestamp, so
/// the header bar drew a different string on every run —
/// [`window_renders_typed_text_under_cage`]'s OCR became STOCHASTIC: the isolated
/// leading `h` of [`OCR_WORD`] misclassified (`ello` / `ﬁello`, the documented
/// signature above) in 2 of ~93 runs. Before that title existed the capture was
/// bit-for-bit reproducible.
///
/// **The fix was not to raise this number.** [`make_temp_cwd`] and [`cage_command`]
/// now hold the rendered title at the fixed string `~/project` for every smoke (a
/// fixed-name cwd under a `HOME` that is its own parent), so the captured page is
/// byte-identical across runs again and the five-file floor measured above still
/// means what it meant when it was measured. Letting a run-varying path back into
/// the title reintroduces that ~2% flake, and raising this count would NOT fix it:
/// more filler files can only make a stochastic page misread less often, never
/// deterministically.
///
/// ## Why it belongs here at all
///
/// Before the per-test temp cwd landed, this smoke ran with `crates/wolf-gnome` as
/// the app's cwd and the sidebar happened to list ~35 repo entries, so the assertion
/// has ALWAYS implicitly depended on that page density — accidentally. Writing these
/// files makes the dependency explicit and deliberate, and mirrors what most other
/// smokes get incidentally from the fixture files they drop into their own temp
/// cwds. (Five drop no fixture files at all: the empty-state smoke hides the sidebar
/// entirely, the `:qa` smoke asserts on an exit status rather than OCR, and the
/// click, drag and scroll pointer smokes build their buffer with `:put` and assert
/// on Neovim-written readback files rather than on pixels.)
///
/// Several smokes now write these fillers DELIBERATELY rather than relying on their
/// own fixtures for density — the keyboard crash-recovery smoke and all three
/// state-persistence smokes, the last of which
/// ([`window_title_shows_the_working_directory_under_cage`]) asserts on a single
/// short header-bar title over an otherwise thin page.
const SIDEBAR_FILLER_COUNT: usize = 9;

/// Names of the [`SIDEBAR_FILLER_COUNT`] marker files. Only the FILENAME is ever
/// rendered (they are listed in the sidebar and never opened), so the names are
/// word-like and all-lowercase — the shapes `tesseract` reads best — and short
/// enough to fit the sidebar pane without being ellipsized.
///
/// The naming constraint is load-bearing. No name may contain [`OCR_WORD`]: a filler
/// row in the sidebar would then satisfy this smoke's assertion on its own, and the
/// test would pass green while the editor drew nothing — a silent false positive.
/// They are likewise kept clear of every other smoke's OCR constants — the asserted
/// SUBSTRINGS, not the fixture filenames: `pickertarget`, `pickone`/`picktwo`,
/// `treenode`, `paneleft`/`paneright`, `htopfile`/`hbotfile`,
/// `focusone`/`focustwo`/`zzleft`, `closekeep`/`closegone`,
/// `triaone`/`tribtwo`/`tricthree`, `folder`, `zzfresh`/`newbufbase`,
/// `quitkeep`/`quitgone`, `chordkeep`/`chordgone`, — added with the
/// pointer-harness unit — `wedgestay`/`wedgeclick`, and — added with the
/// state-persistence unit — `restorealfa`/`restorebravo`/`restorecharlie`,
/// `solozulu`/`soloalfa` and `titlezulu`/`titlehome`, and — added with the
/// buffer-picker unit — `bufpickalfa`/`bufpickbravo`, `responding` and
/// `stallfixture`, and — added with the project-search unit —
/// `projsearchalfa`/`projsearchdelta`, and — added with the Open Recent unit —
/// `ortango`, and — added with the navigation-history unit —
/// `navbackalfa`/`navbackbravo` and `sbtangoleaf`/`sidebarkeepalfa`, and — added
/// with the file-tree-sidebar-parity unit —
/// `cdtango`/`cdrootzulu`/`cdouterhome`, and — added with the Markdown-preview unit
/// — `zzbodyoscar`/`zzothertango`/`zzlivekilo` and the one ORDINARY WORD in the whole
/// table, `preview`, and — added with the preview-interaction unit —
/// `zzunsavedromeo`/`zzcoversierra`, and — added with the terminal-drawer unit —
/// `zzdrawermark`/`zzeditorfill` — so a stray
/// filler can never satisfy one of those either. [`sidebar_filler_names_cannot_satisfy_any_smoke_assertion`]
/// enforces this mechanically against [`ASSERTED_OCR_TERMS`], which holds the consts
/// themselves; this list is a reader's summary and the test is the authority.
///
/// The NATO words `echo` and `hotel` are deliberately SKIPPED: they are the two in
/// the alphabet whose letters are closest to `hello`, and the whole point of this
/// fixture is an OCR classifier that already confuses letter shapes here.
const SIDEBAR_FILLER_FILES: [&str; SIDEBAR_FILLER_COUNT] = [
    "fillalfa.md",
    "fillbravo.md",
    "fillcharlie.md",
    "filldelta.md",
    "fillfoxtrot.md",
    "fillgolf.md",
    "fillindia.md",
    "filljuliett.md",
    "fillkilo.md",
];

/// Body written into every [`SIDEBAR_FILLER_FILES`] entry. Their CONTENTS are never
/// rendered — no scenario opens them — so this exists only so the files are not
/// zero-length.
const SIDEBAR_FILLER_BODY: &str = "# FILLER\n";

/// Picker smoke scenario: the markdown file dropped into the launched app's temp
/// working directory. Its name contains [`PICKER_QUERY`] so the typed query matches
/// it, and its HEADING — deliberately a different word — contains [`PICKER_OCR`] so
/// OCR can confirm nvim actually opened it.
///
/// ## Why the filename must NOT contain [`PICKER_OCR`]
///
/// It used to. This file was named `pickertarget.md` while [`PICKER_OCR`] was
/// `pickertarget`, so the tree sidebar's own row for the fixture satisfied the
/// smoke's positive assertion whether or not the picker ever opened — the editor
/// pane contributed nothing. That is not a theory: during the command-drain unit's
/// broken-accelerator run (GTK's keymap-aware accel matching never fired under
/// `wtype`, so Ctrl+Shift+O was forwarded to Neovim instead of opening the picker)
/// this smoke PASSED while the picker demonstrably never opened.
///
/// It is the exact failure mode
/// [`sidebar_filler_names_cannot_satisfy_any_smoke_assertion`] exists to prevent,
/// which that guard could not catch because it only inspects
/// [`SIDEBAR_FILLER_FILES`], not each smoke's own fixture. The fix follows the
/// precedent already set by [`CLOSE_FILE_GONE`] (`cgone.md` => `# CLOSEGONE`) and
/// [`NEWBUF_FILE`] (`nbase.md` => `# NEWBUFBASE`): put the asserted word in the
/// file's CONTENT and give the file a name whose lowercase does not contain it, so
/// only a rendered buffer can satisfy the assertion. Neither the assertion nor
/// [`PICKER_OCR`] changed — this strengthens the test, it does not weaken it.
const PICKER_FILE_NAME: &str = "ptarget.md";

/// Heading written into [`PICKER_FILE_NAME`]. Once the picker opens the file,
/// nvim renders this line; lowercased, the rendered text contains [`PICKER_OCR`].
/// Deliberately UNLIKE the filename (see the note above), so neither the sidebar
/// row nor nvim's `:e` toast — both of which show `ptarget.md` — can satisfy the
/// assertion on their own.
const PICKER_HEADING: &str = "# PICKERTARGET";

/// Lowercased substring OCR must find in the screenshot to prove the picker
/// opened, matched, selected, and nvim loaded the target file.
const PICKER_OCR: &str = "pickertarget";

/// Text typed into the picker's search entry to filter down to the target file.
///
/// It must match [`PICKER_FILE_NAME`], which is the only file in that smoke's temp
/// cwd: as a prefix of `ptarget.md` it takes the ranker's path-component-prefix,
/// word-prefix and contiguous-substring bonuses, so the target is row 0 and plain
/// `Enter` opens it.
const PICKER_QUERY: &str = "ptarget";

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Keyboard-navigation picker smoke (Up/Down move the list selection).
//
// This scenario proves arrow-key navigation: with TWO files both matching the
// query, pressing `Down` once moves the list selection to the SECOND row, and
// `Enter` then opens THAT row (not the top one).
//
// Deterministic row order (no MRU yet, so order is purely the rank function):
// both files are created fresh in the temp cwd, so neither is in the MRU cache —
// `mru_group`/`mru_rank` tie. With query `pick`, both `pick1.md` and `pick2.md`
// score identically: the single path component (`pick1.md` / `pick2.md`) starts
// with `pick` (+100), the word before the extension (`pick1` / `pick2`) starts
// with `pick` (+20), and `pick` is a contiguous substring (+4) => 124 each. The
// later keys also tie, so the lexicographic `display` tiebreak decides:
//   - score: 124 => tie
//   - match_start: 0 => tie
//   - span: `pick` is contiguous, last-first => tie
//   - display.len: `pick1.md` and `pick2.md` are equal length => tie
//   - display lexicographic: `pick1.md` < `pick2.md` => `pick1.md` is ROW 0,
//     `pick2.md` is ROW 1.
// So Down (one step) selects row 1 = `pick2.md`, whose heading is `# PICKTWO`;
// OCR must then contain `picktwo`. We also (loosely) assert OCR does NOT contain
// `pickone`, proving the selection actually moved off the top row.
//
// Note: this relies on (a) the rank tiebreaks resolving these two equal matches by
// display length then lexicographically (so `pick1.md` is row 0), and (b) `Down`
// moving the LIST/ListBox selection rather than the SearchEntry text cursor. Both
// hold in the current picker; if either ever stops holding, the `picktwo` OCR
// assertion is what catches it.

/// First navigation-test markdown file; sorts BEFORE [`PICKER_NAV_FILE_B`] under
/// the rank tiebreaks (equal-length display, lexicographically smaller), so it is
/// the picker's row 0 for [`PICKER_NAV_QUERY`].
const PICKER_NAV_FILE_A: &str = "pick1.md";

/// Heading inside [`PICKER_NAV_FILE_A`] (row 0). Lowercased it contains
/// [`PICKER_NAV_OCR_ABSENT`]; it must NOT appear after navigating down to row 1.
const PICKER_NAV_HEADING_A: &str = "# PICKONE";

/// Second navigation-test markdown file; the picker's row 1 for
/// [`PICKER_NAV_QUERY`] (see the rank-tiebreak comment above).
const PICKER_NAV_FILE_B: &str = "pick2.md";

/// Heading inside [`PICKER_NAV_FILE_B`] (row 1 — the one we navigate to).
/// Lowercased it contains [`PICKER_NAV_OCR_PRESENT`].
const PICKER_NAV_HEADING_B: &str = "# PICKTWO";

/// Shared query that matches BOTH navigation files so the picker shows two rows.
const PICKER_NAV_QUERY: &str = "pick";

/// Lowercased substring OCR MUST find: the heading of row 1 (`pick2.md`), proving
/// `Down` selected the second row and `Enter` opened it.
const PICKER_NAV_OCR_PRESENT: &str = "picktwo";

/// Lowercased substring OCR must NOT find: the heading of row 0 (`pick1.md`),
/// proving navigation moved the selection off the top row before activating.
const PICKER_NAV_OCR_ABSENT: &str = "pickone";

